
========================================================================
  BUILDING CLASS CATALOGS
========================================================================

  Building n=3...
    2 classes
  Building n=4...
    4 classes
  Building n=5...
    12 classes
  Building n=6...
    56 classes

========================================================================
  PART 1: VERTEX DELETION MAP n -> n-1
========================================================================

  For each class C at n and each vertex v, deleting v gives
  a tournament on n-1 vertices in some class D. The map
  phi_v: classes(n) -> classes(n-1) sends C to D.

  n=4 -> n-1=3:
    class 0 (H=1, sc=(0, 1, 2, 3)) -> children: [0] (H=[1])
    class 1 (H=3, sc=(1, 1, 1, 3)) -> children: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    class 2 (H=5, sc=(1, 1, 2, 2)) -> children: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    class 3 (H=3, sc=(0, 2, 2, 2)) -> children: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])

  n=5 -> n-1=4:
    class 0 (H=1, sc=(0, 1, 2, 3, 4)) -> children: [0] (H=[1])
    class 1 (H=3, sc=(1, 1, 1, 3, 4)) -> children: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    class 2 (H=5, sc=(1, 1, 2, 2, 4)) -> children: [0, 1, 2] (H=[1, 3, 5])
    class 3 (H=3, sc=(0, 2, 2, 2, 4)) -> children: [0, 1, 3] (H=[1, 3, 3])
    class 4 (H=9, sc=(1, 1, 2, 3, 3)) -> children: [0, 2] (H=[1, 5])
    class 5 (H=5, sc=(0, 2, 2, 3, 3)) -> children: [0, 2, 3] (H=[1, 3, 5])
    class 6 (H=9, sc=(1, 1, 2, 3, 3)) -> children: [0, 1, 2, 3] (H=[1, 3, 3, 5])
    class 7 (H=3, sc=(0, 1, 3, 3, 3)) -> children: [0, 3] (H=[1, 3])
    class 8 (H=11, sc=(1, 2, 2, 2, 3)) -> children: [0, 2] (H=[1, 5])
    class 9 (H=15, sc=(1, 2, 2, 2, 3)) -> children: [1, 2, 3] (H=[3, 3, 5])
    class 10 (H=13, sc=(1, 2, 2, 2, 3)) -> children: [1, 2, 3] (H=[3, 3, 5])
    class 11 (H=15, sc=(2, 2, 2, 2, 2)) -> children: [2] (H=[5])


========================================================================
  PART 2: SOURCE-SINK DECOMPOSITION n -> n-2
========================================================================

  The source-sink decomposition removes the highest-scoring
  vertex (source) and lowest-scoring vertex (sink) to get
  an inner tournament on n-2 vertices.

  This is NOT unique: different (source, sink) choices give
  different inner tournaments. We consider ALL such pairs.

  n=5 -> n-2=3:
  12 classes at n=5, 2 classes at n-2=3

  Inner class map (n=5 class -> n-2=3 classes):
    class  0 (H= 1, sc=(0, 1, 2, 3, 4)) -> inner: [0] (H=[1])
    class  1 (H= 3, sc=(1, 1, 1, 3, 4)) -> inner: [0] (H=[1])
    class  2 (H= 5, sc=(1, 1, 2, 2, 4)) -> inner: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    class  3 (H= 3, sc=(0, 2, 2, 2, 4)) -> inner: [1] (H=[3])
    class  4 (H= 9, sc=(1, 1, 2, 3, 3)) -> inner: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    class  5 (H= 5, sc=(0, 2, 2, 3, 3)) -> inner: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    class  6 (H= 9, sc=(1, 1, 2, 3, 3)) -> inner: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])
    class  7 (H= 3, sc=(0, 1, 3, 3, 3)) -> inner: [0] (H=[1])
    class  8 (H=11, sc=(1, 2, 2, 2, 3)) -> inner: [1] (H=[3])
    class  9 (H=15, sc=(1, 2, 2, 2, 3)) -> inner: [1] (H=[3])
    class 10 (H=13, sc=(1, 2, 2, 2, 3)) -> inner: [0] (H=[1])
    class 11 (H=15, sc=(2, 2, 2, 2, 2)) -> inner: [0, 1] (H=[1, 3])

  Fiber sizes (n-2 class -> how many n-classes sit above):
    inner class 0 (H=1) has fiber of size 9: [0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 11] (H=[1, 3, 5, 9, 5, 9, 3, 13, 15])
    inner class 1 (H=3) has fiber of size 8: [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11] (H=[5, 3, 9, 5, 9, 11, 15, 15])

  n=6 -> n-2=4:
